            "type": "concept",
            "title": "James-Lange: the body goes first",
            "body": "In 1884 William James flipped common sense — and Carl Lange independently reached the same idea a year later: you don't tremble because you're afraid, you feel afraid because you tremble. The emotion is your mind reading your body's reaction. Strip away the racing heart and tensed muscles, James argued, and nothing of the fear remains.",
