            "body": "Water takes about 4.18 joules per gram to warm by one degree, roughly nine times what iron needs. Hydrogen bonds soak up the energy before the molecules can speed up. That is why coastal cities have mild winters, why the ocean absorbs most of the planet's extra heat, and why a boiled pan stays hot long after the ring is off.",
