            "type": "example",
            "title": "Sounds You Stop Hearing",
            "body": "Janet Werker and Richard Tees, 1984: English-learning babies of six to eight months easily tell apart consonants used in Hindi and in Nlaka'pamux, a Salish language spoken in British Columbia — contrasts adult English speakers cannot hear at all. By ten to twelve months, the same babies no longer can.",
            "art": "wave"
