            "type": "concept",
            "title": "Bracketing the Truth Claim",
            "body": "Scholars call it methodological agnosticism. When a tradition says the Quran was revealed, or that the Buddha reached awakening, the researcher records the claim, traces what follows from it, and sets the verdict aside. Not because the question is unimportant — because this particular tool cannot settle it.",
